      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "The maximum distance between two opposite vertices of the rectangle is 10+11=21, so the distance between opposite vertices of the triangle must not exceed 21/2=10.5.  Thus, the triangle has sides of length 10, 10, and 10.5.  We can use Heron's Formula to find the area.  The semi-perimeter is s=10(10+10.5)/2=35.25.  The area is then A=$\\sqrt{s(s-10)(s-10)(s-10.5)}$=$\\sqrt{35.25(35.25-10)(35.25-10)(35.25-10.5)}$=$\\sqrt{35.25(25.25)(25.25)(24.75)}$=94.142 or 94.142 (to the nearest hundredth).\n\nThus, the maximum possible area of such a triangle is \\boxed{94.14} square units.\n\nThis problem revolves around the maximum distance that can be extended from one vertex of a rectangle to one vertex of an equilateral triangle that fits inside the rectangle.  There is some misdirection about where the equilateral triangle has been drawn - do not be fooled into thinking it has been drawn symmetrically on the rectangle.  You can prove that the triangle has sides of lengths 10, 10, and 10.5.  Make a triangle using the lines through opposite points of ABCD.",
