      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "When side of the equilateral triangle cut the rectangle so that it exactly cuts up the corners of the rectangle, the possible area of triangle is found.\n\n11×10÷2=55\n\nWhen side of the equilateral triangle cut the rectangle so that there is  small portion of the rectangle cut up, the area of the triangle gradually decreases.\n\n$\\frac{11×10}{2}-\\frac{11×(10-44)}{2}=44$\n\nThe perimeter of the triangle is 55. Substituting this:\n\n\nThe longest side of the triangle is 22. The rest sides can be found using the Pythagorean Theorem.\n\nSince the base $A_1DI_1A_2$ of the triangle is 11, it follows that the height $h$ is $\\sqrt{22^2-11^2}=20$\nThe remaining sides will have area 16 unit. In order to find the area of the whole triangle we will use the area formula of a triangle.\n\n\n36 + 200 + 16 = 252\n\n\nSo the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle problem is $\\boxed{252}$.",
