      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that fits within a rectangle with sides of lengths 10 and 11, we start by identifying the different possible dimensions of the equilateral triangle within the rectangle.\n\nFirstly, consider the case where the equilateral triangle is aligned such that one of its vertices is at a corner of the rectangle. Let's assume the side length of the equilateral triangle is \\( s \\). In this scenario, the side of the triangle will align with one of the sides of the rectangle, say, one vertex touching the 10-unit side and one vertex touching the 11-unit side. The height of an equilateral triangle with side length \\( s \\) can be calculated using the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Height} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s. \\]\nThis height must be less than or equal to 11 (since it cannot exceed the side length of the rectangle).\n\nTherefore, we have the inequality:\n\\[ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\leq 11. \\]\nSolving for \\( s \\), we get:\n\\[ s \\leq \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{22 \\sqrt{3}}{3}. \\]\nWe shall use \\( s = \\frac{22 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\) to maximize the area of the triangle. The area \\( A \\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\( s \\) is given by:\n\\[ A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2. \\]\nSubstituting \\( s = \\frac{22 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\), we get:\n\\[ A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{22 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{484 \\cdot 3}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1452}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{464}{3} = \\frac{464 \\sqrt{3}}{12} = \\frac{116 \\sqrt{3}}{3}. \\]\nThus, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{116\\sqrt{3}}{3}}. \\]",
